# JoystickPushTask — Project Plan (condensed 9 Oct 2026)

Head-fixed mouse 1D forelimb object-push task, forked from the Mathis joystick-pull LabVIEW rig. This file is force-tracked (path is in `.gitignore`, but edits still commit). Keep one copy only. Older detailed notes live in git history. Where this file disagrees with older notes, this file wins.

## Safety rules (always)
- Class 3B 635 nm laser on `PFI3` / terminal 42: keep keyed OFF. Never toggle PFI3 blind. Do not touch the laser pulse nodes in the magnet loop.
- Never power the Ledex coil from AO0 (AO0 is only the 0–10 V command; PCIe-6321 AO limit ±5 mA).
- 1 A inline fuse must stay in the 12 V positive lead of the PVA/Ledex supply. Do not use the magnet's reserved 12 V Goobay supply for anything else.
- Normal Stop only; toolbar Abort is emergency-only (skips cleanup frames and the auto timing file).
- Never press an animal's paw onto the pad. Keep `mag = 0` until the unperturbed task is stable.
- Never hand-edit `.vi` binaries or internal names. Do not Git-merge independently edited `.vi` files; the repo copy is the canonical hierarchy.

## Working conventions
- Two machines share this repo through GitHub `master`: the rig PC (Windows, LabVIEW, hardware, data) and a Mac (planning, docs, parameter files, Python scripts, CAD). Pull before editing; commit and push after each finished step. Commits use the `maxchalabi` identity and imperative sentence messages ending in a period; no Cursor co-author trailer (a local commit-msg hook strips it).
- The VIs are edited by hand in LabVIEW on the rig PC, so guide changes one small step at a time (which node, which wire, how to check it) and have the user confirm before moving on. Agents never edit `.vi` binaries. Prefer parameter files, front-panel controls and Python/scripts over diagram changes where possible.
- After every step, update this file with the new status (and keep it short: replace stale items, do not append logs). The repo is public: keep this file free of private chat, people's names and purchasing/admin details.
- Bench-test everything animal-free first; one variable at a time; no improvising circuit changes during an animal session.
- Parameter files must be validated after editing: exact row count, 27 tab-separated columns per row, consistent row definition.

## Rig and DAQ
- NI PCIe-6321, SCB-68A breakout (terminal map in `RIG_INVENTORY.md`). Saved NI-MAX tasks are incomplete; audit inline channels. NI-MAX backup: `ni-max/JoystickPushTask_NIMAX_2026-08-20.nce`.
- Channels:
  - AI task `joysticklickframe_push`, continuous 10 kHz, order `ai1` (Y = push, white, queue idx 0), `ai0` (X = lateral, red, idx 1), `ai2` (rest pad, RSE 0–5 V). `joystick pos` = Y, X, 0, rest pad (idx 3, the slot the lab DataJoint layout calls FSR).
  - AO0 `MagnetPush_Dev1` (Ledex via PVA); AO1 `LickSpout_Dev1` (Actuonix L12-30-50-12-I, 0–5 V, own 12 V PSU).
  - DO `Water_Dev1` P0.0; `SuccessCue_Dev1` P0.1 (terminal 17, D GND 15; Adafruit #1536 buzzer).
  - `frame counter_Dev1` on PFI8 (ctr0); pulse out ctr1 on PFI13 at 50 Hz. Camera runs on the rig PC.
  - Rest pad terminals: +5 V terminal 8, `ai2` terminal 65, AI GND terminal 64.
- `avg joystick and frame trig lick3.vi` acquisition loop: Read stays **10** samples (1 kHz logged), input buffer **100000** installed, in-loop `stop in` Value property node deleted, `stop all` global (`PushTask Globals.vi`) drives all loops. Earlier -200279 overrun is provisionally fixed; a 25–30 min no-animal run with rewards, stopped cleanly, is still to be reported. Do not switch Read to 100.
- Do not run two simultaneous AI tasks (one AI timing engine). 1 MΩ source impedance ghosts `ai0` onto `ai2` on this multiplexed ADC.
- Data on the rig: `C:\Users\Mathis_Lab_1\Desktop\RIG1_DATA` (`joystick_`, `REWARD_`, `TRIAL_`, `EVENTS_`, `TIMESTAMPS_LABVIEW_`). Python 3.6.4 at `C:\Users\Mathis_Lab_1\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python36-32\python.exe`.

## Current status (18 Oct 2026)
- **Rest-pad sensor (capacitive, installed and bench-validated 14 Oct).** Arduino Nano (classic ATmega328, not Nano Every) + 10×10 mm copper-tape pad under one layer of tape + 10 kΩ pot (sensitivity knob). Sketch `arduino/touch_pad/touch_pad.ino`, wiring and calibration in `arduino/touch_pad/README.md`. Output is push-pull 0 V / 5 V on D2 into `ai2` (terminal 65; 5 V = touch; low impedance, no X ghosting); Nano powered from rig +5 V (terminal 8), GND to AI GND (64); unplug USB when running from the rig. `rest pad threshold` 2.5 V (default saved in the VI, `3fe0e0f`); Case 0 starts only with pad touched AND joystick home. Re-tare the knob (hands away) after any change to the pad or its cover. Normal Stop clean; 25–30 min no-animal run done.
 - **50 Hz chatter (found in mouse session 1, 16 Oct).** With the paw on the pad, `ai2` toggled every ~20 ms (high ~13 ms, low ~8 ms; 10,572 touch episodes, only 87 ≥ 100 ms) whenever the knob put the threshold near the paw signal (mostly from minute 16 on, when trial starts stopped). The period drifts against the DAQ clock, so it is mains hum picked up through the mouse, not the camera pulse train. The knob cannot fix it. Sketch fix uploaded, bench-tested and reinstalled on the rig (16 Oct, `ai2` ~0 / ~5 V in NI-MAX): readings averaged over exactly one mains period (20 ms, decision every 10 ms), `OFF_FRACTION` 0.5, touch hold 20 ms, release hold 40 ms, Serial Plotter `ripple` field. New timing: touch-on ~30–50 ms, release ~50–60 ms. `archive/restpad_chatter.py joystick_<name>` (moved from `scripts/` 18 Oct) reports chatter per session (session 1 baseline: 210 chatter seconds, 0.8 % of episodes ≥ 100 ms).
 - Mains-filter sketch confirmed in mouse sessions (16 Oct, zuchon and blackmamba): 0 chatter seconds, touch episodes median 1.2–1.7 s, ≥ 99 % of episodes ≥ 100 ms (anaconda, old sketch: 210 chatter seconds, median 13 ms). Pad sensitivity, sketch and position are final. Deferred: check with the Fluke whether the metal head-fixation clamp is connected to AI GND 64; if not, add one wire clamp → 64 and compare `ripple` with and without (do this if chatter remains).
 - Open items: water-drop/licking check on the rig with the spout extended; pad lead is ~40 cm plain wire (target <30 cm); shielding deferred (2-photon first, ephys uncertain; compare imaging noise with the sensor on and off at the first imaging session).
 - Data impact: joystick-file column 4 (`ai2`, `joystick pos` idx 3) is a ~0 V / ~5 V touch signal (switch lag ~30–50 ms on, ~50–60 ms off with the mains-filter sketch; no force information); file layout unchanged. DataJoint must treat it as Boolean for new sessions and record the sensor type (`fsr402` or `capacitive`) per session; no other DataJoint change needed (column renamed fsr→rest pad).
 - Rejected for good: AT42QT1010 (recalibrates after ~60 s), Omron E2K-F (too big), M12/M18 industrial sensors, load cell (fallback only), SingleTact and FlexiForce. Earlier FSR 402 history: `archive/FSR_rest_pad_history.md`.
- **Mice (back on water restriction Wed 14 Oct).** Thu 15 Oct: ~25 min head-fixation per mouse at 4 µl rewards (habituation to the smaller volume; earlier 8 µl). Fri 16 Oct: first rest-pad hold + micro-push session for 3 mice (numbers below from the files; 4 µl rewards):
 - `anaconda` (`02_micro_push_day1_restpad.txt`: home 2.47–2.56, success < 2.47, `home TO` 100, ITI 2000; 29 min): 37 rewards / 161 trials (23 %), 31 of them in the first 15 min; trial starts stopped after ~20 min (old sketch, 50 Hz chatter). Home lower bound = success bound, so trials starting at ~2.47 V succeeded on noise (2 rewards with a 3 mV excursion). In 28/37 rewards the pad stayed touched during the push (other paw, or paw on cube and pad together, cube was in the middle hole).
 - `zuchon` (`_home2p44.txt`: home 2.43–2.52, success < 2.43, ITI 2000; 28 min; `_2` is a 10 s false start, discard): 4 rewards / 85 trials, all four the intended sequence (pad released 150–390 ms after trial start, then push). Joystick/cube moved during the session (home drifted 2.45 → 2.47 V; needed ~40 mV push). From minute ~11 the paw never returned to the pad (no touch for the last 16 min) and kept pushing the cube (45–52 push bouts per 5 min, 197/204 outside trial windows): cube too close.
 - `blackmamba` (`_home2p475.txt`: home 2.475–2.51, success < 2.47, `home TO` 50, ITI 1500; final position; 34 min): 19 rewards / 250 trials (7.6 %; user recalled ~37), rising later (14 after minute 17). 12/19 rewards with the pad released first (0.1–2.8 s), 7 with the pad touched throughout (check video). 169/231 failed trials: paw rested on the pad for the whole 3 s window. 179 push bouts (median 78 mV, ≫ the 16 mV needed), only 29 inside trial windows; most outside pushes had no pad touch in the second before (paw went to the cube without a pad hold). All 250 rows of the file were used (last trial at 34.0 min).
 - Next: next session in the final position with `_home2p475` settings but a longer file (≥ 500 rows; check what the VI does past the last row) and consider `move time` 3000 → 5000–8000 ms (pushes starting within 3 / 5 / 8 s of trial start in blackmamba: 17 / 28 / 40 trials). Check video for pad-touched successes. Water earned is small (≈ 150 / 16 / 76 µl), so supplement to the daily allowance.
 - Data issues: zuchon's magnet loop missed trial 84 (after a 328 s pause: no code 20/21, TRIAL file 84 rows vs 85 trial starts in EVENTS), so `make_labview_timing.py` refuses that session; anaconda and blackmamba timing files build (drift +4.5 ppm, worst mismatch 6–8 ms). Find the cause (magnet-loop Wait on Occurrence) before any nonzero `mag`. **Fixed joystick/cube position (16 Oct, final after session 1 adjustments): the front face of the cube is 6 mm from the edge of the platform and 7 mm from the edge of the rest pad (the pad sits on the platform); reuse these distances on any other rig or mount.** Changes made during/after it: the cube is mounted in the hole nearest the rest pad (not the middle hole) so the paw cannot touch cube and pad together; the rest pad moved slightly to the right so only the right paw can reach it. Remeasure home voltage after these moves. Stage-1 procedure, if used instead: use only `deliverWater_RIG1_cue.vi` (50 ms cue then water), `Water on time (ms)` 240 (~8 µl), extend spout to 3.0 V before the first reward and retract to 0 V at the end (that VI does not move the spout). Keep the push VI closed. Cap 25 min and the approved water limit; stop when licking fades; tally rewards. Pad-contact shaping fallbacks `01_pad_contact_shaping_50ms.txt` / `_250ms.txt` (`home TO` 50 / 250 ms, otherwise identical) are untested with the capacitive pad; do a bench check first. First pilot (28 Sep) and shaping attempts showed contact activation was too rare for contingency learning.
- **Ledex `195224-230` + PVA 1.6 (0–10 V)**, following `MAGNET_COMMISSIONING.md`. Checkpoints 1–4 DONE (11 Oct), Checkpoint 5 (mount and direction) waits for the printed holder (next week).
  - Wiring (verified): 12 V Goobay + → 1 A ESKA fuse (TC-R3-32AA1 holder) → `D`, − → `F`; SCB 22 → `H`, 55 → `G` (twisted); coil on `C`/`A` (no polarity); Enable `J` on a wire with a clip that is clamped on the `D` screw to enable and removed to disable. `K`, `M`, `E` empty. Supply 11.91 V, LED red when disabled. Leave the supply unplugged and the clip off between tests.
  - Trimmers (left to right, from the datasheet legend): rising ramp, falling ramp, Imax (3), Imin (4), dither. Imin and Imax are ~30 turns counterclockwise (slip clutch, no hard stop); counterclockwise = minimum confirmed for Imin (≤ 0.006 A at 0 V with Enable on).
  - Coil current vs AO0 (plunger seated, cold, 2–3 s pulses, Fluke 10 A DC in series): 0.5 V 0.053, 1 V 0.074, 2 V 0.106, 3 V 0.121, 4 V 0.147, 5 V 0.180, 6 V 0.201, 8 V 0.273, 10 V 0.310 A. No sound or heat, clean release. 0.58 A cannot be exceeded at this Imax, so no command clamp is needed for current; Imax stays at minimum for now (raise clockwise in small steps only if more force is needed).
  - Pulse method used: set ao0 in NI-MAX Test Panels (Analog Output tab, `Dev1/ao0`, never `ao1`) with Enable off, clip on `D` for 2–3 s, unclip, back to 0 V, 10 s off. Measure H–G resistance only with the SCB end disconnected (a live ao0 makes the Ω reading fluctuate). Move the Fluke red lead back to `VΩ` after current tests.
  - Plunger: 60° (cone-ended) type; the cone end goes inside against the matching taper and the plain pull end protrudes (tip still protrudes when seated); never reverse it, never run it loose. Without the plunger no pull could be felt even at 10 V. Design chosen: plunger linked to the joystick shaft (see "Plunger link"); the washer target is rejected (re-checked on the measured rig, 15 Oct).
 - Datasheet (STA 20 × 40 mm pull, 30 AWG, 1491 turns; PDF and digitized 60° curves in `simulations/ledex_femm/datasheet/`): seated holding force 12.8 N (60°) at 0.57 A; plunger pull at 0.57 A 5.35 / 3.38 / 2.14 / 0.78 N at 1.5 / 3 / 5 / 10 mm stroke. Seated pull end is 23.81 mm beyond the body face (M14 boss 9.52 mm on the plunger side). Nothing is published for an external target.
 - Ledex force models (`simulations/ledex_femm/`, README there; FEMM 4.2 in `C:\femm42` on the rig and under Wine on the Mac; `femm_run.py` runs it in parallel, `calibrate.py` fits it). FEMM (2D) calibrated against the datasheet curves (12 Oct): error ±15–20 % incl. a held-out curve; still 1.4–2× high beyond 8 mm stroke. One Ledex geometry for all models: `simulations/ledex_femm/ledex_geometry.py` (body, plunger, slot, cross hole; shared by the reference CAD, rig model, FEMM and 3D). FEMM uses axisymmetric stand-ins for the slot and cross hole (plunger force changed ≤ 2 %, calibration error 0.178 → 0.180, kept); the 3D model now uses the real slotted, cross-drilled tip (washer pull: short towers +15–25 %, tall towers −7 %). Washer forces come from the 3D model (`fea3d.py`, NGSolve venv `/tmp/ngenv`, set `FEA3D_THREADS` when running sweeps in parallel; `sweep3d.py`, incl. the `offset` group: tower moved vertically, half model); treat as ±40 %. Previous results are in `results/*/axisym_tip/`. Holder axis range 38–78 mm is one setting (`HOLDER_H_RANGE` in `rig_geometry.py`) used by the CAD, forces and explorer. Mathis 2017 gives no force at the joystick (replaced the stock ~4 N base spring to get 0.1–0.2 N = 10–20 g).
  - **Rig geometry (15 Oct; `rig_geometry.py`, README "Rig geometry").** Built from the measured joystick component and component holder CAD (`mechanical/joystick-component/`, `joystick-holder/`); world X = push (+ away from the mouse), Z up from the breadboard, origin under the joystick axis. Shaft base Z 43.18 (screw head glued in the shaft's bottom bore, screw into the stub, cone seated on the stub top), constrainer 15.41–71.41 on the holder frame; its home-wall arch (top Z 63.41) is cut square up to the ceiling Z 68.41 in the window revision (`joystick-1d-axis-constrainer/make_ledex_window.py`, print that one), cube contact Z 135.6. Pivot assumed mid-body, 23.5 mm below the shaft base (bounds 19.3–27.3); rig check: push the cube 10 mm against a ruler, r = shaft travel at height z / 10, pivot depth = (92.4 r − z)/(1 − r). The shaft is in the cube's front hole (push face 2.05 mm from the axis, `CUBE_HOLE` in `rig_geometry.py`; forces recomputed 16 Oct: change < 1 %; models switched to the narrow cube 17 Oct: effective mass 4.2 g, forces unchanged). A 15 mm push tilts the shaft 7.4°; lever predicts ~0.49 V per 10 mm push vs the provisional 0.30 V reading; remeasure. The constrainer slot's home end stops the shaft 1.15 mm (at the cube) on the home side of the spring's neutral, so the magnet pulls the handle onto that stop at home (home shifts ~0.06 V on `ai1` with the magnet on): move the stop to neutral (slot end Y −1.5 or a 0.5 mm shim) or record it.
  - **Plunger link (chosen design; `plunger_link.py`, `rig_forces.py`, README "Plunger link" and "Washer target vs plunger link").** Rigid 14 mm link (1.8 × 5 mm bar in the plunger's 2.5 × 9.5 mm tip slot) pinned through the plunger's factory 2.5/2.6 mm cross hole 4.76 mm from the tip (datasheet F34; nothing to drill; slot turned vertical, 2.5 mm × 10 mm brass/A2 pin) to a collar fork pin 6 mm off the shaft axis on the home side (collar: ring with 3.7 mm bore, fork, one M2 set screw on +Y; nothing may stick out on +X, it would hit the constrainer near 15 mm). Ledex body and plunger modelled from the drawing in `mechanical/ledex-195224-model/` (plunger 59.7 mm long, mesh masses match the datasheet). Link height limits: shaft z 15 to 20.3 (window top); height barely changes force. Recommended: link z 17.5 = Ledex axis Z 60.7, home stroke 3 mm at neutral = body face X −42.05; moving parts clear by ≥ 2 mm from the home stop to 15 mm (bar play 0.35–0.4 mm in the slot and fork); stroke 3.0 → 8.35 mm over 15 mm. Resistance at the cube, push 0 → 15 mm: 1 V 2.3 → 0.64, 5 V 13 → 3.8, 10 V 40 → 11, 0.58 A 125 → 39 gf (pivot bounds −7/+6 %). Washer target at its best layout (Z 58.7, 1 mm gap): 10 V 0.75 → 0.06 gf, i.e. the link is ~53× stronger. Spring: fitted, no preload (force = k × push, k adjustable); plunger friction at the cube is 1.2 / 2.3 / 3.0 gf (mu 0.15 / 0.3 / 0.4), leaving a dead band where k × push < friction. Model at mu 0.3: k 0.2 gf/mm never returns (stops at +8.4 mm), 0.5 gf/mm returns in ~60 ms, 0.67 gf/mm in ~48 ms; measure the real k on the bench (gram scale). The explorer has no preload slider (removed 18 Oct). Its sliders show physical distances rather than world coordinates: **stroke length (mm)** (plunger stroke from seated at neutral, 0 = fully seated; link option) or **distance to target (mm)** (gap to the washer tower at neutral; washer option), **magnet height (mm)** (height distance above the pivot), and **pivot depth (mm)** (relative to the bottom face of the TS-1D1S00A component, 2.0–10.0 mm, default 5.77 mm). All sliders span the feasible range (recomputed from the feasibility checks; beyond a 12.5 mm stroke the pull is extrapolated) and can also be typed. Moving the pivot depth changes only the magnet-height reading and range, never the magnet position. A "set default" button resets every slider (layout, pivot, push, drive, spring, friction). A button switches all forces (readout, plots, stiffness) between gf and N (stiffness as N/m). Push at the cube runs from the home stop to 20 mm (the slot would allow ~36 mm at the cube). Explorer: `figures/rig/rig_explorer.html` (all parts, sliders, live forces, design map; force plots use linear gf axes, lateral Y fixed at 0).
  - Final install rules: PVA outside the mouse box; command wires 30–100 cm twisted; coil cable 1–2 m twisted and away from `ai0/ai1/ai2`; short pulses and long cool-downs; stop on heat, smell, binding, wrong direction or failure to release; re-check 0 V and a small command after moving anything. Random-force sessions need this proportional driver (not a binary switch).
  - **Adjustable Ledex holder (15 Oct; `mechanical/ledex-holder-adjustable/`, generated by `make_ledex_holder.py`, not printed).** Base with two M6 slots along X (33 mm travel, 25 mm or 1 inch grid), upright with lateral M4 slots (±12.75 mm) and two long vertical M3 slots (axis Z 38–78, 1 mm scale), carriage on the M14 boss held by 4 × M3 above and below the case into captive nuts (adjustable from outside with the Ledex fitted); link collar (gap and stroke gauges archived in `archive/ledex-gauges/`, 17 Oct, and removed from the generator) and bar, `fit_test` (print first: holes print ~0.3–0.4 mm small, cube's 3.4 mm holes just pass the 3 mm shaft; allowances in the `FIT` block). Clearances to the joystick holder/constrainer: link layout ≥ 16 mm, washer layout ≥ 4.3 mm; `make_ledex_holder.py check` reruns the static and moving checks.
  - Next: caliper-check the plunger (slot width, cross hole Ø and distance from the tip, seated protrusion 23.81, length) and the M14 nut; print `fit_test` (adjust `FIT` if needed), then the window constrainer, holder, collar and link, fix the home stop, then bench checks without mice: spring curve with a gram scale at the cube (0 / 5 / 10 / 15 mm, with and without the plunger, Ledex off; preload ≥ 2× the difference), release test 10× from 15 mm back to home within ±0.035 V on `ai1`, hard stop holds at 10 V with clean release at 0 V, scale force at 5 V and 10 V at a 5 mm push to calibrate the model; then Checkpoint 5 (spring return, no binding, release at 0 V). LabVIEW magnet safety (guaranteed 0 V on every exit path, logging) still to do before any nonzero `mag`. Spare: Bürkert 316532 (due 2 Dec) if not cancelled.
- **Rest-pad release clause (built and diagram-reviewed, bench test pending, 19 Oct).** Problem in session 1: `move time` started at the Case 0 pass while the paw stayed on the pad, so the mouse started many trials it never completed. Case 0 next state is now **5**; new **Case 5** waits for paw release only (`ai2` = `joystick pos` idx 3 < `rest pad threshold`, read local variable; joystick ignored); on release it writes Tick Count → `trail start` (the `move time` clock, moved from Case 2), logs new EVENTS **code 6** (paw release), then → Case 1. Case 2 keeps Set Occurrence (magnet loop: `Trial #` + TRIAL row) and code 3 at the start zone, so `make_labview_timing.py` (code 3 must equal TRIAL samples) is unaffected. Case 1 keeps the legacy start box = the "start position" (later halfway between home and end); paw re-touch does not reset. Before making the start box nontrivial, Case 1 needs a `move time` timeout → Case 0 (write current time to `start time`, log code 5, set `in trigROI` F), otherwise failure to reach a separated start zone would wait forever. Bench test with `parameters/training/02_micro_push_day1_restpad_home2p44-2p49.txt`: home 2.44–2.49 V held for 100 ms, full-range start box 0–5 V (irrelevant), success 0–2.35 V. Next: animal-free bench test (finger held on pad: no trial; lift: code 6 then trial start; lift + push: reward; movement timeout back to Case 0; Normal Stop), then record result. Data impact: code 6 marks paw release + ~50–60 ms sensor lag; code 2 values now include 5.
